From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Morinaga <me@gustavomorinaga.dev>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:22:13 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] draw engine: a converted-vertex cache; a repeated draw is not
 fetched, converted and staged again

After ps3recomp pull request #718 by n0madic (MIT), the engine side and its
bookkeeping as proposed there; the Metal backend's half is left out (the
Vulkan one is patch 2063). The draw call itself is merged by hand around
set_draw_key, which this tree has and upstream does not.

Killzone HD repeats 66-71% of its ~750 draws a frame byte for byte from this
frame or the last (index bytes, layout, attribute state and vertex bytes),
and converting them costs the RSX thread 1.1-1.4 ms a frame on the rebased
Level 1 and Level 7 routes. A draw's compact vertices and rebuilt indices
are kept in GPU-readable chunks, keyed by a 128-bit hash of everything they
are made from; a changed byte is another key, so nothing is invalidated, and
entries nobody uses age out. A block is handed out again only after the
submission that last read it has completed (the backend's commit_serial).
Draws the key cannot describe (immediate or inline vertices, per-instance
attributes, sparse ranges) are staged as before. PS3RECOMP_ENG_VCACHE=0
turns the cache off; PS3RECOMP_ENG_VCACHE_STATS=1 prints its hit rate.
Added here: PS3RECOMP_ENG_VCACHE_VERIFY=1 converts every hit again, compares
it with the stored copy byte for byte (vertices, indices, counts, topology)
and draws the fresh conversion when they differ, counting and logging it
(rsx_vc_chunk_cpu reads a stored copy back).

Tests: libs/video/tests/test_vertex_cache.c (hash, buddy allocator, deferred
reuse, eviction) and test_engine_vertex_cache.c (the engine through a stub
backend: hits, a changed vertex byte, index or format converts again, the
switch and a backend without the hooks stage as before; the verify mode
passes an equal copy and catches a corrupted one), both run by
tools/test_rsx_vertex_cache.py.
